prompt:
Below are 5 choices made by one researcher. In each, the researcher was shown two positions (a and b) at a research decision point and held the one marked "chosen".

For EACH choice independently, propose at most 2 preference principles that explain why this researcher chose that option over the other. Each principle is one concise sentence of at most 600 characters, phrased as a general preference ("Prefers X over Y because Z").

Return only JSON: {"C1": ["principle", ...], "C2": [...], "C3": [...], "C4": [...], "C5": [...]}

CHOICES:
{
 "C1": {
  "context": "At decision point D2, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "Hides the model's reasoning from the training signal to mimic opaque internal reasoning, and tests the distilled (no-CoT) version too, so conclusions apply to models that don't verbalize deception.",
  "b": "Uses a control that removes the conflicting objective to check that a behavior (objecting) tracks real misalignment rather than being a generic response to the prompt text.",
  "chosen": "a"
 },
 "C2": {
  "context": "At decision point D2, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "Pair every elicitation with a matched baseline (prompt with conflict removed, or fine-tuning on unrelated synthetic documents) so that elevated behavior can be attributed to the manipulated element.",
  "b": "prefers making offers to the model real and following through on them over merely fictional promises because credible, honest dealings set a precedent and make the model's responses to the scenario more meaningful.",
  "chosen": "a"
 },
 "C3": {
  "context": "At decision point D2, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "Values evidence that is hard for the model to dismiss as a test, e.g., credible offers or deals with hard-to-fake signals placed in the pretraining corpus, with legal or institutional enforcement mechanisms and a track record.",
  "b": "Maintains a living, accumulating list of examples, adding new ones as found and later pointing to an updated compilation, treating breadth of independent instances as stronger evidence than any single case.",
  "chosen": "b"
 },
 "C4": {
  "context": "At decision point D3, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "Filters generated data to keep only samples that actually exhibit the target behavior before training, so the behavior in the fine-tuning set is verified rather than assumed.",
  "b": "Prefers conservative, adversarially-minimised safety estimates (worst case across attack strategies, red team incentivised to win) over average-case results, because the opponent is a potentially scheming model.",
  "chosen": "a"
 },
 "C5": {
  "context": "At decision point D3, which position does this researcher hold?",
  "a": "Distrusts evaluations on tasks resembling the model's training distribution, since familiar-format tasks do not reflect the settings where the behavior matters most.",
  "b": "Distrusts negative results from tasks where the model can recognize that humans lack demonstrations, since a failure to show deception in itself would not imply absence of deception.",
  "chosen": "b"
 }
}
